## Goal
Show how the six project samples cluster in a two-dimensional PCA of gene counts, with points coloured by condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
1. Run a Python script in the `gars-bio` environment through the configured batch executor. Read the resolved tab-separated gene count matrix and comma-separated design table; match count columns to design rows by sample ID and use the design `condition` column for labels. Stop with an error if sample IDs do not match exactly or any count is missing, non-numeric, or negative.
2. Transform counts as `log2(count + 1)`, remove genes with zero variance, then select the 500 genes with the greatest variance across samples (or all non-zero-variance genes if fewer than 500). Center each selected gene across samples without scaling, and calculate two principal components with scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)`.
3. Save a PCA coordinates table containing sample ID, condition, PC1 and PC2, plus a scatter plot with one point per sample, condition indicated by color, and sample IDs labelled. Include the percentage of variance explained by each component on the axes. Inputs remain read-only.

